g to do but it also can translate  into sales as well  now when we're going into uh next gen  we're seeing a lot more awareness of  accessibility and whether that's in part  because the last of us part two this  year  or just the momentum in general our  conversations are  becoming more prominent and a majority  of people know about accessibility  and a lot of those people are developers  and they want to be able to try to make  their games  available and for everyone in recent  years game developers and designers have  been more proactive about improving  accessibility  and reaching out to advocates like steve  who worked on the last of us part 2  has been key to that however  fundamentally each console has its own  accessibility challenges that many don't  think about and require more involvement  from platform holders and the industry  at large  to address in a twitter thread earlier  this year  steve called the 100 difference in price  between the disc and digital editions of  the ps5  are disability tax citing the  playstation store's inflexible refund  policy  is an issue for many gamers with  accessibility requirements  obviously there are two different  versions of playstation 5 you have the  digital and the disk version  generally a lot of disabled players  usually prefer to  purchase the physical copy of a game  mainly because a lot of the refund  policies  from if you purchase the game digitally  are not at a place yet  where they can be able to at least get  their money back because often times  whenever we have to be able to put down  like 60 dollars plus that's a lot of  money  for for some people to spend on a game  and if a disabled player  pays that amount for a game they take it  home and  they play it and they realize an hour in  i can't play this that's a lot of money  to be able to kind of uh  sort of in a way be wasted and if you  buy it digitally  oftentimes the refund policy is well  you already downloaded it so we can't  give you a refund  whereas if we buy it physically at least  we could be able to  take it back to the store and even  though we can't get a full refund  we more likely can be able to trade it  in to be able to at least get some of  our money back and when it comes to  sort of the disability tax i had  mentioned with the ps5  it basically costs uh in some cases a  hundred dollars more  to be able to have access to that also  as well the ability to be able to use  game pass as a selling point for the new  consoles is  a huge accessibility advantage because  like i mentioned regards to  sort of the disability tax what's great  about game pass  is that you can be able to get games day  one of release from xbox first party  studios  and when you have the be able to get  those games by paying a certain amount  per month you could be able to download  those games day one  and you can play it try it out if it's  if it doesn't work for you and you're  not able to play it  you can easily delete it download a new  one and  you're not wasting a huge amount of  money for it as consoles move towards an  all digital future  and with accessibility features rarely  included in a game's marketing prior to  launch  subscription services such as xbox game  pass become  all the more important in ensuring that  gamers with individual requirements  don't end up unfairly out of pocket  playstation does offer playstation now  but its library is by comparison  skewed much more towards older games and  unlike game pass  does not generally include new first  party titles upon release  there's also the playstation plus  collection but once again this consists  of last gen games  until accessibility features are common  as part of a game's marketing  or sony changes their refund policy to  account for accessibility  it may continue to be gamers with those  requirements who foot the bill  there are also considerations to be made  when it comes to the hardware itself  it's a mundane point but a larger  heavier console or one that requires you  to attach a stand with a small screw  means that many people with physical  disabilities may find themselves unable  to unbox and set up their new console  without the assistance of someone else  which given the world is currently in  the midst of a pandemic  is far from ideal a headline feature for  the ps5 is the brand new dualsense  controller which features haptic  feedback and adaptive triggers  but these settings can be disabled on a  system level as well as individually in  titles such as mars morales  which offers accessibility vibration  frustratingly these additions mean that  the dualshock 4 and other ps4 compatible  controllers  are not supported for ps5 games which  will be a concern to those who have a  personalized setup  adapted to old hardware by contrast  microsoft has ensured that any  compatible xbox one generation  controller works without issue on the  series x and s  as with the xbox one you can remap  buttons on a system level  and the xbox adaptive controller is  supported along with the adaptive gaming  kit  though microsoft deserves ploridix for  being the only major company with an  official adaptive controller and the  only one selling additional third-party  solutions on its site  it should be noted that the controller  and kit could set you back over 200  dollars  not including the external joysticks  that are required for the xbox uh series  x and s  what microsoft did was they actually  kind of took a lot of what they had  already developed internally and refined  it  for next generation so if you're coming  from an  xbox one and you're using the  accessibility settings uh  in the ease of access menu in the xbox  it's the exact same  ease of access menu on the series x and  s  and so you're going to get the same  experience that's on the xbox side on  the playstation side actually what they  did was  kind of interesting pretty much the same  settings that is on  playstation 4 is on the playstation 5.  they have also  kind of enhanced a lot for blind players  before  there was a text-to-speech option in the  playstation 4  that unfortunately was only available in  english only but now with the  playstation  5 they've enabled screen reader to be  available  in multiple languages across the world  so  you can be able to turn on the console  for day one when you get it and it will  guide you through the setup process and  you can be able to keep that on  throughout the rest of your setup fast  too fast three  and it's an amazing way to be able to  kind of navigate the system and it works  flawlessly  across the entire operating system of  the playstation  voice type voice volume and then when it  comes to sort of like the dual sense  controller that i have here  uh with the haptic feedback it basically  adds a lot more immersion for blind  players as well  where the precise and subtle details of  the vibrations in the controller  actually help blind players to be able  to feel the environment that they're  in and know when they're walking or when  they're  being attacked when things are kind of  moving from side to side  and that along with sort of like the 3d  audio that's built into it is  a really amazing feature and actually  playstation has a  neat little feature in it called game  presets where any game you play will  automatically turn on  whatever difficulty setting you want to  be able to set whether to turn on  subtitles  or to invert controls and enable  settings that you normally would have to  turn on whenever you start up a game for  the first time i'm really excited to be  able to see  what else they can that sony and  microsoft can be able to do  moving forward now that accessibility in  a way is kind of built in  from from the ground up it's clear the  industry has come a long way  in the past 10 years in recognizing the  challenges that some people face  just to be able to play games at all but  hopefully this is the beginning of a  progression towards 